@page "/docs/design-system"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>Design system · Lux.UI</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Design system</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">Seven semantic colors that you map to palettes, a neutral palette behind the text, background and border tokens, and global defaults for color and size. The default look is tuned by hand for contrast.</p>

    <h2>Semantic colors</h2>
    <p>Components take a <code>Color</code> by role, never by shade. Each role is a triplet of CSS variables (<a href="/docs/css-variables">the full list</a>) that components read through <code>data-color</code>.</p>
    <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-default" data-testid="semantic-table">
        <table class="w-full min-w-xl text-start text-sm">
            <caption class="sr-only">Semantic colors</caption>
            <thead class="bg-muted text-highlighted">
                <tr><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Color</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Used for</th><th scope="col" class="px-3 py-2 text-start">Default palette</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Roles)
                {
                    <tr class="border-t border-default">
                        <th scope="row" class="px-3 py-2 text-start font-mono text-xs font-medium text-highlighted">@row.Name</th>
                        <td class="px-3 py-2 text-muted">@row.Use</td>
                        <td class="px-3 py-2 text-muted">@row.Default</td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Choose the palettes</h2>
    <p>Map a role to any of the 26 Tailwind CSS v4 palettes (<code>LxPalette.Blue</code>, <code>LxPalette.Zinc</code>…), in <code>AddLuxUi</code>, with the same call on the server and in the WebAssembly client. A role you leave alone keeps the Lux.UI default; <code>LxPalette.Lux</code> puts it back.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Configure" />
    <p>Then render <code>LxThemeStyle</code> in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, next to the color mode script and before your stylesheet. It writes one <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> with the <code>:root</code> and <code>.dark</code> variables, and renders nothing while the colors are the defaults, which <code>lux.css</code> already contains. It is plain markup, so it is the same in static rendering, prerendering and every interactive mode.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="App.razor" Code="@Head" />

    <h2>How the tokens are picked</h2>
    <p>A palette has 11 shades and a component needs three tokens that stay readable, so Lux.UI picks them from the shades and checks the result with the same rules its own palette passes: the fill reaches 3:1 on the page and the surfaces (except the light primary, whose <code>-text</code> color outlines it, as the signal yellow does), the text on the fill 4.5:1, also at the 90% and 80% of the hover and pressed states, and the <code>-text</code> color 4.5:1 on the page and over soft tints.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The <strong>fill</strong> is the shade nearest to 500 in light mode and 400 in dark mode that satisfies the rules; a pale palette such as yellow moves a step or two.</li>
        <li>The <strong>contrast</strong> color is white or the palette's shade 950, whichever reaches 4.5:1 (white first).</li>
        <li>The <strong>text</strong> color is the nearest shade to 600 (light) or 400 (dark) that holds on every surface.</li>
        <li>The <strong>neutral</strong> palette also drives the text, background and border variables from its shades (700 for text, 500 muted, 50 for <code>bg-muted</code>…), and a color left at the default is nudged where the new surfaces need it. Borders start at 200 in light mode and 800 in dark mode and move toward more contrast until they differ by at least 1.15:1 from the page, the overlay, the muted and the elevated surfaces, so a border never disappears on a popover or a card (the Lux.UI defaults reach 1.17:1 in light and 1.19:1 in dark).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Your own palette</h2>
    <p>Give all 11 shades, as <code>#rgb</code>, <code>#rrggbb</code> or <code>oklch(L C H)</code>, written as in CSS Color 4: <code>L</code> is a number from 0 to 1 or a percentage, <code>C</code> a number or a percentage (100% is 0.4), <code>H</code> a number of degrees or a value with <code>deg</code>, <code>grad</code>, <code>rad</code> or <code>turn</code>. A missing shade or a color that does not parse (<code>NaN</code>, <code>Infinity</code>, an unknown unit…) throws an <code>ArgumentException</code> naming the palette and the value when the palette is created.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Custom" />
    <p>When no combination of shades can meet the contrast rules, for example with a palette whose shades are all the same gray, resolving the theme (the first <code>LxThemeStyle</code> render) throws an <code>InvalidOperationException</code> naming the color, the palette and the rule that fails, instead of shipping a low-contrast theme. Set <code>o.Colors.AllowLowContrast = true</code> to keep the best combination found anyway.</p>

    <h2>Extra semantic colors</h2>
    <p>Register any custom semantic role name and use it as a <code>Color</code>. <code>LxThemeStyle</code> writes its three variables and its <code>[data-color='tertiary']</code> rule automatically — no hand-written CSS required.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Extra" />
    <CodeBlock Code="@ExtraUse" />

    <h2>Palettes, live</h2>
    <p>This block renders the palettes of its own options into a scope with <code>LxThemeStyle Colors Scope</code>, so you can see the tokens move, in light and in dark mode. A scope receives every token in both modes, even with the default colors, so the preview is exact inside a page that configured other colors. It needs interactivity to switch.</p>
    <div class="not-prose">
        <Example Of="typeof(Examples.ThemeStyleExample)" Title="Primary palette" />
    </div>

    <h2>Default color and size</h2>
    <p>Like <code>theme.defaultVariants</code>, <code>Defaults.Color</code> replaces <code>Primary</code> where a component uses it by default, and <code>Defaults.Size</code> replaces <code>Md</code>. A parameter you set always wins, and the components that are neutral by design (icon buttons, separators, key caps) stay neutral.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Program.cs" Code="@Defaults" />

    <h2>Which components follow the defaults</h2>
    <p>A component follows <code>Defaults.Color</code> or <code>Defaults.Size</code> when its built-in value is the library default, <code>Primary</code> or <code>Md</code>, and the parameter is not set. Some keep a fixed value on purpose.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Follow Defaults.Color:</strong> <code>LxButton</code>, <code>LxBadge</code>, <code>LxChip</code>, <code>LxAlert</code>, <code>LxBanner</code>, <code>LxProgress</code>, <code>LxStepper</code>, <code>LxTimeline</code>, <code>LxTree</code>, <code>LxPagination</code>, <code>LxTabs</code>, <code>LxNavigationMenu</code>, <code>LxCarousel</code>, <code>LxSplitter</code>, <code>LxFileUpload</code>, <code>LxAuthForm</code>, <code>LxLocaleSelect</code>, <code>LxColorModeSelect</code> and <code>LxColorModeSwitch</code>, every form field, <code>LxChatPrompt</code> and its send button, <code>LxContentToc</code> and <code>LxContentNavigation</code>, <code>LxEditorToolbar</code> and <code>LxEditorDragHandle</code>, and the page family: <code>LxPageCta</code>, <code>LxPageFeature</code>, <code>LxPageCard</code>, <code>LxBlogPost</code>, <code>LxPricingPlan</code>, <code>LxPricingTable</code> and <code>LxChangelogVersion</code>. The badge of a blog post or a changelog version, the buttons of a pricing table and the buttons built from <code>PageLinkItem</code> follow it unless a color is set on them.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow Defaults.Size:</strong> <code>LxButton</code>, <code>LxBadge</code>, <code>LxChip</code>, <code>LxAvatar</code>, <code>LxAvatarGroup</code>, <code>LxUser</code>, <code>LxKbd</code>, <code>LxProgress</code>, <code>LxStepper</code>, <code>LxTimeline</code>, <code>LxEmpty</code>, <code>LxPagination</code>, <code>LxLocaleSelect</code>, <code>LxColorModeSelect</code>, <code>LxColorModeSwitch</code>, <code>LxColorModeAvatar</code>, <code>LxColorModeButton</code>, <code>LxFileUpload</code>, <code>LxChatPromptSubmit</code>, <code>LxChatAttachment</code>, <code>LxContentSearchButton</code>, the dashboard sidebar toggle and collapse, and every form field (inside a <code>LxFormField</code>, the field's own size comes first).</li>
        <li><strong>Neutral by design, so they ignore Defaults.Color:</strong> <code>LxKbd</code>, <code>LxSeparator</code>, <code>LxColorModeButton</code>, <code>LxContentSearchButton</code>, the dashboard sidebar toggle and collapse, <code>LxChatMessage</code> and <code>LxChatAttachment</code>. The chart series use their own <code>--lux-chart-N</code> colors, which fall back to the semantic colors.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed size by design, so they ignore Defaults.Size:</strong> <code>LxModal</code>, <code>LxContainer</code>, <code>LxIcon</code>, <code>LxSeparator</code> and the editor menus and toolbar. The page components size their own buttons (large in a hero, a section or a call to action, medium in a header).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Without LxThemeStyle</h2>
    <p>The tokens are plain CSS variables: the same colors can be written by hand, in <a href="/docs/css-variables">:root and .dark</a>, with or without Tailwind. The options only compute them for you and keep the contrast rules.</p>
    <p>Everything is configured in <code>AddLuxUi</code>, which runs before the first render. The colors and defaults become read-only the first time a component or <code>LxThemeStyle</code> uses them, like a resolved theme, and a later change throws an <code>InvalidOperationException</code>.</p>
</div>

@code {
    private sealed record Role(string Name, string Use, string Default);

    private static readonly Role[] Roles =
    [
        new("primary", "Main actions, focus, selected state.", "Signal yellow"),
        new("secondary", "Secondary actions.", "Teal"),
        new("success", "Success states.", "Green"),
        new("info", "Informational states.", "Blue"),
        new("warning", "Warnings.", "Orange"),
        new("error", "Errors, destructive actions.", "Red"),
        new("neutral", "Text, backgrounds, borders and neutral components.", "Cool gray"),
    ];

    private const string Configure = """
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o =>
        {
            o.Colors.Primary = LxPalette.Blue;
            o.Colors.Neutral = LxPalette.Zinc;
            o.Colors.Add("tertiary", LxPalette.Indigo);
        });
        """;

    private const string Head = """
        <head>
            <LxColorModeScript />
            <LxThemeStyle />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Lux.UI/lux.preflight.css"]" />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Lux.UI/lux.css"]" />
        </head>
        """;

    private const string Custom = """
        var brand = LxPalette.Custom("brand", new Dictionary<int, string>
        {
            [50] = "#f0fdfa", [100] = "#ccfbf1", [200] = "#99f6e4", [300] = "#5eead4", [400] = "#2dd4bf",
            [500] = "#14b8a6", [600] = "#0d9488", [700] = "#0f766e", [800] = "#115e59", [900] = "#134e4a",
            [950] = "#042f2e",
        });
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o => o.Colors.Primary = brand);
        """;

    private const string Extra = """
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o => o.Colors.Add("tertiary", LxPalette.Violet));
        """;

    private const string ExtraUse = """
        <LxButton Label="Share" Color="@("tertiary")" />
        <LxBadge Label="New" Color="@("tertiary")" Variant="LxVariant.Soft" />
        """;

    private const string Defaults = """
        builder.Services.AddLuxUi(o =>
        {
            o.Defaults.Color = LxColor.Secondary;
            o.Defaults.Size = LxSize.Sm;
        });
        """;
}
